Article 11 (Rulings on Whether to Grant Permission to Institute Lawsuits)
(1) A written ruling granting or denying permission to institute a lawsuit shall state the following matters and bear the name and seal of the judge making the ruling:
1. The plaintiff and the plaintiff's litigation representative;
2. The defendant;
3. The operative part of the ruling;
4. The grounds for the ruling.
(2) The grounds of a ruling denying permission to institute a lawsuit shall identify the requirements for permission that have not been met.
(3) A ruling granting permission and a ruling denying permission to institute a lawsuit shall be served on the plaintiff and the defendant by providing a certified copy of the ruling.
(4) Where a ruling denying permission to institute a lawsuit becomes final, the organization lawsuit shall be deemed not to have been instituted.
